+# Agent Note: Name the /proc process-tree platform set
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-12-name-the-proc-process-tree-platform-set.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Two readers in `dsh-subprocess-local` compared the target platform against the `'linux'` literal: the platform dispatch in `createProcessInspector`, and the process-group liveness rule that `spawn.ts` runs after the direct child settles. Neither comparison named what the reader actually required, and the two copies had to stay in step by hand.
+
+Android is Linux-family. Bionic serves `/proc/<pid>/stat` and `/proc/<pid>/task` in the layout these readers parse, and `os.platform()` reports `android` there, so a supported reader was excluded by a platform string rather than by a missing capability. The exclusion was not benign: terminal inspection threw `terminal inspection is unsupported on platform android` at plugin load instead of reading the process table.
+
+## Decision
+
+`process-inspector.ts` exports `hasProcProcessTree(platform)`, and both readers call it.
+
+The predicate is named for the requirement — a Linux-style `/proc` process tree — rather than for an operating system, and it answers `true` for `linux` and `android`. The platform set is stated once, next to the readers that depend on it.
+
+`createProcessInspector` keeps its fail-loud `throw` for every other platform, so an unsupported platform still stops at plugin load rather than degrading into an inspector that reads paths it cannot parse.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+- **Repeat `platform === 'linux' || platform === 'android'` at each reader.** Keeps the platform set in two places and still names no requirement, so the next reader added for the same `/proc` tree has to rediscover the set.
+- **Widen the predicate to a general POSIX check.** macOS is POSIX and serves no `/proc` process tree; it has its own inspector and its own liveness behavior. A general POSIX predicate would route macOS readers to the Linux implementation.
+- **Probe for `/proc` at runtime.** A probe reports the container or sandbox view of the moment, so a masked `/proc` would silently move the terminal inspection path at runtime instead of failing at load. The platform is already known statically.
+- **Compare against `os.platform()` inside the predicate.** Callers pass a target platform explicitly, including platforms other than the host, so the predicate stays a pure function of its argument.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Terminal inspection and process-group liveness work on Android with no Android-specific code in either reader. Unsupported platforms keep their explicit error.
+
+A third `/proc`-backed platform is added by changing one predicate. A platform that serves `/proc` under a different layout must still add its own reader, because the predicate states which platforms share the layout, not which ones have a `/proc` directory.
